-- ===========================================================================
-- 0004_supplier_hang_placement — WHERE an injected hang sits, not just how long
-- it lasts.
--
-- Generated by drizzle-kit from packages/db/src/schema/supplier.ts and then
-- hand-edited, exactly as 0002 was and for exactly 0002's reason: drizzle-kit
-- emitted
--
--   ALTER TABLE "supplier_behaviour" ADD COLUMN "hang_before_claim" boolean NOT NULL;
--
-- which cannot run. `supplier_behaviour` already holds the two seeded rows `a`
-- and `b`, and a NOT NULL column with no default has no value to give them:
--
--   23502 not_null_violation
--   column "hang_before_claim" of relation "supplier_behaviour" contains null values
--
-- So the column arrives with `DEFAULT false` and the default is dropped in the
-- next statement — 0002's pattern for `issuance_attempts.attempt` and
-- `supplier_requests.provider`, and the whole of §10's "why DROP DEFAULT
-- matters".
--
-- WHAT THIS BUYS
--
-- Spec 003 technical-considerations §7.1 and architecture.md §5 record two hang
-- scenarios that need OPPOSITE orderings, and a single hang point in the stub
-- can only produce one of them:
--
--   Slow but successful   hang BEFORE the key claim,  hang_ms < SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS
--                         a slow supplier is not a failed one; the call
--                         completes normally and nothing times out.
--
--   The timeout trap      hang AFTER the claim COMMITS,
--                         SUPPLIER_TIMEOUT_MS < hang_ms < function ceiling
--                         a key genuinely issued, a client that timed out and
--                         cannot know it, and a re-probe on the same request_id
--                         that gets the same code back.
--
-- The DURATION half of each scenario was already expressible — `hang_ms` is a
-- column. This is the PLACEMENT half, and without it "both are worth having and
-- they are different checks" is not a thing a reviewer can arrange without
-- rebuilding the API, which is the one thing functional spec §2.7's fourth
-- criterion forbids: *reproduced without changing the shop itself*.
--
-- WHY A BOOLEAN AND NOT A `hang_at` ENUM (or a text column with a CHECK)
--
-- There are exactly two places a hang may go, and that is a fact about the
-- code rather than a simplification: the key claim and its ledger write are ONE
-- transaction (SupplierKeyClaimService.claimAndRecord), so a hang is either
-- before that transaction or after it commits. The only third value anybody
-- would ever reach for is INSIDE it — and that one must never exist, because it
-- holds the instance's single pooled connection (packages/db/src/client.ts,
-- `max: 1`) for the whole of hang_ms and stalls every other request in the
-- process, including the re-probe the trap is staged to observe. A two-valued
-- column cannot express it. An enum would offer it a name and a place to sit.
--
-- It also keeps this table's shape: five knobs whose "off" is a zero, and now a
-- sixth whose "off" is `false`. `hang_before_claim` is named for the
-- EXCEPTIONAL placement on purpose — the control endpoint replaces the whole
-- row, so an omitted field takes the seeded baseline, and the baseline has to
-- be the scenario a reviewer arming a bare `hang_next` actually wants. That is
-- the trap. A `hang_after_claim` column would have had to seed `true`, i.e. a
-- baseline row that is not all-zero, which packages/db/src/fixtures/supplier-behaviour.ts
-- argues against at length.
--
-- MIGRATION SAFETY. Metadata-only in both statements. Since PG 11 an
-- `ADD COLUMN ... NOT NULL DEFAULT <constant>` stores the value in
-- `pg_attribute.attmissingval` and does NOT rewrite the table; `false` is a
-- constant, not a volatile expression. Measured on this project's Postgres 16
-- container, three runs inside a rolled-back transaction, against the two
-- seeded rows:
--
--   ADD COLUMN    13.5 ms / 2.0 ms / 2.6 ms   (first run pays catalogue cache)
--   DROP DEFAULT   3.8 ms / 1.0 ms / 1.1 ms
--
-- The `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock both statements take is on `supplier_behaviour`
-- alone — the supplier's own knobs, which no shop path reads — and is held for
-- those few milliseconds.
--
-- ATOMICITY, restated because 0002 had to correct the record: drizzle-orm's pg
-- dialect opens ONE transaction spanning every pending file and every
-- `drizzle.__drizzle_migrations` insert, not one per file. Do not reason as
-- though this file commits independently of 0003.
--
-- ORDERING. Depends on 0003 (the table) and on nothing else; nothing depends on
-- it. Slice 4's indexes are not in this file.
--
-- SEEDING. `packages/db/src/seed.ts` writes the baseline from
-- `packages/db/src/fixtures/supplier-behaviour.ts`, which now carries
-- `hangBeforeClaim: false`, under ON CONFLICT (provider) DO NOTHING. A database
-- that already has its two rows is backfilled by the DEFAULT below rather than
-- by the seed — which is why the DEFAULT is here at all — and a fresh clone
-- gets the same `false` from the fixture. The two agree because the seed and
-- the control endpoint's reset read the one constant.
-- ===========================================================================

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- `false` means AFTER THE CLAIM COMMITS — the trap — and `true` means before
-- it. The stubs read this in the same statement that spends `hang_next`
-- (apps/api/src/suppliers/supplier-behaviour.service.ts, `consumeOneShot`):
--
--   UPDATE supplier_behaviour SET hang_next = hang_next - 1, updated_at = now()
--   WHERE provider = $1 AND hang_next > 0
--   RETURNING hang_next, hang_ms, hang_before_claim;
--   -- 1 row  => THIS call hangs, for THAT long, in THAT place. All three facts
--   --           come out of the one statement that spent the counter, so a
--   --           concurrent PUT cannot move the placement between the decision
--   --           and the wait.
--   -- 0 rows => none left; fall back to hang_rate. NOT an error.
--
-- The DEFAULT exists only to give the two existing rows a value, and is dropped
-- immediately: §10's rule, and the same argument as `issuance_attempts.attempt`.
-- Left in place it would let a future half-written INSERT arm a placement
-- nobody chose and look deliberate doing it; without it that insert takes a
-- 23502 instead.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ALTER TABLE "supplier_behaviour" ADD COLUMN "hang_before_claim" boolean NOT NULL DEFAULT false;--> statement-breakpoint
ALTER TABLE "supplier_behaviour" ALTER COLUMN "hang_before_claim" DROP DEFAULT;
